{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher tumor mutational burden (TMB) is associated with increased expression of immune checkpoint genes PDCD1, CD274, and CTLA4 in SKCM tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether tumors with more somatic mutations express higher levels of key immune checkpoint genes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. In the mutation data, count total high‐confidence somatic mutations per patient to define TMB. 3. In the expression data, map each sample to its patient (first 12 characters of TCGA barcode) and, if multiple samples per patient exist, average their expression values. 4. Identify the Ensembl IDs for PDCD1, CD274, and CTLA4; extract each gene’s expression per patient. 5. Merge TMB and gene expression tables on patient_id. 6. For each checkpoint gene, compute Spearman correlation between TMB and expression; adjust p‐values for three tests (e.g., Benjamini‐Hochberg). 7. Fit linear regression models: gene_expression ~ TMB + age_at_diagnosis to control for age. 8. Visualize scatter plots of TMB vs expression for each gene, overlaying regression lines and reporting correlation coefficients and adjusted p‐values.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of TMB per patient.  \n• Patient‐level expression values for PDCD1, CD274, CTLA4.  \n• Spearman correlation coefficients and adjusted p‐values for each gene.  \n• Regression summaries with effect sizes and p‐values.  \n• Scatter plots with regression lines and annotations."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher TMB is associated with improved overall survival in SKCM patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether mutation burden predicts better survival outcomes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Compute TMB per patient by counting somatic mutations. 3. In clinical data, define survival time (days_to_death if dead, else days_to_last_followup) and event indicator (1=Dead, 0=Alive). 4. Merge TMB and survival data on patient_id. 5. Stratify patients into high‐TMB and low‐TMB groups by median split. 6. Generate Kaplan–Meier curves for the two groups and perform log‐rank test. 7. Fit a multivariate Cox proportional hazards model with continuous TMB, age_at_diagnosis, and ulceration_indicator as covariates. 8. Check proportional hazards assumptions via Schoenfeld residuals.",  
      "deliverables":"• Median TMB and group counts.  \n• Kaplan–Meier plot with log‐rank p‐value.  \n• Cox model table with hazard ratios, 95% CIs, and p‐values.  \n• Diagnostics for proportional hazards assumption."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"Primary tumor location influences MITF expression, with skin‐origin tumors exhibiting higher MITF levels than lymph node metastases.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test if MITF, a melanocyte lineage marker, is differentially expressed by tumor site (Skin vs Lymph nodes).",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. Extract primary_site for each patient and filter to two groups: 'Skin' and 'Lymph nodes'; drop others or missing. 3. Map expression samples to patients and average if multiple. 4. Identify MITF Ensembl ID; extract its expression per patient. 5. Merge primary_site and MITF expression tables. 6. Compare MITF expression between Skin and Lymph nodes groups using Wilcoxon rank‐sum test. 7. Plot boxplots or violin plots of MITF by site with p‐value annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Counts of patients in each site group.  \n• Summary statistics of MITF expression by group.  \n• Wilcoxon test statistic and p‐value.  \n• Boxplot or violin plot annotated with p‐value."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"Breslow thickness correlates positively with MMP9 expression in SKCM tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether tumors with greater vertical thickness express higher levels of the invasion‐associated gene MMP9.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. In clinical data, select patients with non‐missing breslow_thickness. 3. Map expression data to patients (average if multiple samples). 4. Identify MMP9 Ensembl ID; extract expression per patient. 5. Merge Breslow thickness and MMP9 expression tables. 6. Compute Spearman correlation between breslow_thickness and MMP9 expression and test significance. 7. Create a scatter plot with a fitted smooth curve (e.g., LOESS) and annotate correlation coefficient and p‐value.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of Breslow thickness and MMP9 expression per patient.  \n• Spearman correlation coefficient and p‐value.  \n• Scatter plot with trend line and annotation."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tumor proliferation marker MKI67 expression correlates with histologic mitotic count per mm² in SKCM patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Validate the relationship between molecular marker MKI67 and pathologist‐reported mitotic count as measures of proliferation.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. From clinical data, select patients with non‐missing mitotic_count_per_mm2. 3. Map expression data to patients and average replicates. 4. Identify MKI67 Ensembl ID; extract expression. 5. Merge mitotic_count and MKI67 expression. 6. Perform Spearman correlation test between mitotic_count_per_mm2 and MKI67 expression. 7. Generate scatter plot with regression line and display correlation and p‐value.",  
      "deliverables":"• Patient‐level table of mitotic count and MKI67 expression.  \n• Spearman correlation coefficient and p‐value.  \n• Scatter plot with fit line and annotation."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients who received prior radiation therapy have higher tumor mutational burden than those who did not.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if radiation exposure prior to tumor sample collection is associated with increased somatic mutation counts.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv. 2. From clinical data, extract prior_radiation_therapy status (YES/NO) and drop missing. 3. In mutation data, count mutations per patient to compute TMB. 4. Merge prior_radiation_therapy and TMB per patient. 5. Compare TMB distributions between YES and NO groups using Wilcoxon rank‐sum test. 6. Summarize group medians and perform effect‐size calculation (e.g., Cliff’s Delta). 7. Visualize groups with boxplots or violin plots annotated with p‐value.",  
      "deliverables":"• Counts of patients by radiation status.  \n• Group medians and interquartile ranges of TMB.  \n• Wilcoxon test statistic, p‐value, and effect size.  \n• Boxplot or violin plot with annotation."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"SKCM tumors harboring NRAS mutations exhibit higher CD274 (PD‐L1) expression compared to NRAS wild‐type tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether NRAS mutational status influences expression of the immune checkpoint ligand PD‐L1.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. Flag patients with any NRAS mutation in Hugo_Symbol; define NRAS‐mutant vs wild‐type groups. 3. Map expression data to patient level (average if multiple). 4. Identify CD274 Ensembl ID; extract per‐patient expression. 5. Merge NRAS status and CD274 expression. 6. Compare CD274 expression between groups using Wilcoxon rank‐sum test. 7. Plot boxplots with p‐value annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Counts of NRAS‐mutant and wild‐type patients.  \n• Summary statistics of CD274 expression by group.  \n• Wilcoxon test statistic and p‐value.  \n• Boxplot annotated with p‐value."  
    }  
  },  

  {  
    "hypothesis":"Advanced AJCC pathologic stage (III/IV) at diagnosis is associated with higher CD274 (PD‐L1) expression than early stage (I/II) tumors in SKCM.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Investigate the relationship between tumor stage and immune checkpoint expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. From clinical data, extract ajcc_pathologic_stage_raw; parse and group into early (I, II) vs advanced (III, IV), drop missing or ambiguous. 3. Map expression samples to patient level. 4. Identify CD274 Ensembl ID and extract expression. 5. Merge stage group and CD274 expression. 6. Compare expression between early and advanced groups using Wilcoxon test. 7. Create boxplot or violin plot with p‐value annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"• Counts of patients in early vs advanced stage groups.  \n• Summary of CD274 expression by stage group.  \n• Wilcoxon test statistic and p‐value.  \n• Plot with annotation."  
    }  
  }  
]}
